        "note": "<div class=\"Example\"><table class=\"example\"><tr><td><p><i>āp</i></p></td><td><p>2nd pl. hon.</p></td><td><p>- second person singular and plural honorific</p><p>reference;</p><p>- honorific pronoun with highest degree of respect:</p><p>seniors; case marking is different if used as a reflexive</p><p>pronoun</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><i>tū</i></p></td><td><p>2nd sg. intim.</p></td><td><p>- characterizes intimacy and informality, also contempt</p><p>or disgust; is used in reference to small children and</p><p>gods</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><i>tum</i></p></td><td><p>2nd pl. hon.</p></td><td><p>-second person singular and plural honorific reference;</p><p>used to address relatives, close friends, low social</p><p>status people such as servants</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><i>tum</i></p></td><td><p>2nd sg. hon.</p></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><p><i>ve</i></p></td><td><p>2nd sg. hon.</p></td><td><p>- honorific 2nd person</p><p>reference is not demonstrated</p><p>with data, source is 3rd person pl. absolutive remote</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><i>ye</i></p></td><td><p>2nd sg. hon.</p></td><td><p>- honorific 2nd person reference is not demonstrated</p><p>with data, source is 3rd person pl. absolutive</p><p>proximate</p></td></tr></table></div>",
